\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation_core}
  \uses{def:Martingale, def:LocallyBoundedVariationOn, lem:BoundedVariationOn.sq_increment_sum_tendsto_zero_of_uniform_bound}
  \lean{MeasureTheory.Martingale.eq_zero_of_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation_core}
Let \(M\) be a real-valued true martingale with \(M_\bot=0\).  Fix a
deterministic time \(t>\bot\).  Assume that on the deterministic horizon
[\(\bot,t]\) the paths are continuous, have bounded variation, and are bounded
by a deterministic integrable constant.  Then \(M_t=0\) almost surely.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Choose finite deterministic partitions
\(\pi_n=\{\bot=s^n_0\le \cdots \le s^n_{k_n}=t\}\) whose mesh tends to zero.
For a martingale, increments over disjoint deterministic intervals are
orthogonal in expectation: if \(a\le b\le c\le d\), then
\[
  \mathbb E[(M_b-M_a)(M_d-M_c)] = 0.
\]
Indeed \(M_b-M_a\) is \(\mathcal F_b\)-measurable and integrable, while the
martingale property gives
\(\mathbb E[M_d-M_c\mid \mathcal F_b]=0\) when \(b\le c\).
Expanding the square of the telescoping sum over a partition therefore gives
\[
  \mathbb E[M_t^2]
  =
  \sum_i \mathbb E[(M_{s^n_{i+1}}-M_{s^n_i})^2].
\]

For each sample point, continuity on \([\bot,t]\) implies that the largest
increment along \(\pi_n\) tends to \(0\).  Bounded variation on the same
horizon and
Lemma~\ref{lem:BoundedVariationOn.sq_increment_sum_tendsto_zero_of_uniform_bound}
then imply that the pathwise square-increment sum tends to \(0\).  The
deterministic boundedness of the stopped process supplies an integrable
dominating random variable for these square-increment sums.  Dominated
convergence sends the right-hand side above to \(0\), so
\(\mathbb E[M_t^2]=0\).  Since \(M_t^2\ge0\), the standard nonnegative-integral
criterion gives \(M_t^2=0\) almost surely, hence \(M_t=0\) almost surely.
\end{proof}


